A||N||20||varnster||calling all audio nuts||||1||
Z||000000||varnster||12-22-2000||12:11 AM|||| Im installing some ear candy in the 87 gt<BR>and just want some ideas for placement of the<BR>amp, its a big boy! 4 channel sony xm 752eqx<BR>{75x4} 15" wide x 2" high x 12" diameter.<BR>Really putting some $$ in this system for show, so I want it to be tucked away, but still Im wanting it to jump out and say here<BR>I am. maybe in the front after taking out the spare? but I hate to go with no spare.<BR>any suggestions?||||reg||1
Z||000001||william_thorniii||12-22-2000||08:45 AM||||varnster,<P>Don't take this the wrong way... but the best place to put a Sony amp is back in the store where you bought it.  I worked in a home audio shop and knew of the EXTREMELY poor quality of Sony home audio amplifiers and I had always heard the stories about how bad Sony car amps were.  I could not pass up the price on one so I tried a 75Wx4 amp.  It could not even power my Polk 6.5" component set... bridged!  Absolutely the worst amp I have ever used.  I would guesstimate that RMS is more likely producing 15-25 Watts.  My MTX PRO75x4, although an entry level amp as far as competition audio is concearned, has ample power for both my 10 Polk db sub and the 6.5" component set.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||12-22-2000||09:15 AM||||WOW, I have a Sony 4X75W and it's messed up too!<BR>It started having "turn on" problems after about a year of use. You would turn on the head unit and it would play for about 5 seconds then the amp would shut down. After about 4-5 times of cycling the head unit off and on the amp would finally stay up. Lasted about another month then I couldn't get it to stay on. Swapped in a Pioneer amp, no problems.<P>As for amp location, Mine are behind the passenger and driver side seats along with crossovers and surround sound processor.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||12-22-2000||11:01 AM||||back before i got a fiero i saw fierosounds gt in car stereo rewiew. i've more or less copied his amp install in various ways. worth checking on.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||12-22-2000||01:15 PM||||Behind the seat is where I mounted my Rockford Fosgate amp. Good place for an amp.||||reg||1
Z||000005||MachineHead||12-22-2000||01:25 PM||||Definately drop that horrible amp.  How much did you pay for it, There are better amps in that price range.  What are you trying to push with that amp?<P>If you want a cool spot to show off your amp, I would say:<BR>1)  Install a lid over the spare and put the amp on the lid kinda.<BR>-----------------or----------------<BR>2)  intstall it where that center console thingy is on the rear wall.<P>If you have no clue as to what I'm talking about, let me know and I will try to explain it better.||||reg||1
Z||000006||william_thorniii||12-22-2000||02:07 PM||||Machinehead,<P>Regarding your second proposal, of locating an amp on the center console.  Do you think it would be safe to locate an amplifier on top of the ECM?  I would worry about the EMF making the car go screwy.  <P>The only important thing about amplifier placement is to ensure that the area is well ventilated.  If you seal it up in an enclosure, make sure you use a powered exhaust fans (like in your computer) in the enclosure.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000007||BAM-BAM||12-22-2000||02:24 PM||||  Get ahold of FieroSound, he has an award winning setup I think you'll like. <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierosound/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierosound/</A> <P> Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach/KYB Susp.<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats,headliner! MOMO Commando Steering Wheel, White Face Race Style Gauges And Soon To Be More!!! YEAH BABY-YEAH||||reg||12
Z||000008||varnster||12-22-2000||03:26 PM||||Thanks for all the replys, Ive been using <BR>this particular amp in my 97 explorer it<BR>was pushing my 10" cerwin vega and the frount<BR>and rear polks Ive had really good use from<BR>this amp for over a year. Im only changing it out because my wife got me an ORIAN 4 channel. sorry you 2 guys had probs, with yours, AUDIOPHILE MAG gave this amp high praise, considered a best buy. thats why I<BR>got it. Thanks again, maybe you should look<BR>at your install methods before trashing an<BR>entire line of amps.||||reg||1
Z||000009||goldfinger||12-22-2000||04:27 PM||||Possible places for amps could be In the back part of the car, or behind the seats are the best places. I am putting 2 subs and my amp behind my seats. I have a sony deck and it sucks, I don't like sony Mobile Audio products. Everyone has there opinion.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||12-22-2000||05:04 PM||||I like Sony stuff, but for the price, there are better car audio amps than Sony...and much worse. On the other hand, I stuck a cheap Profile California Series amp in my Suburban 3 years ago and was ridiculed by my friends for buying a POS amp that wouldn't last (I didn't want to spend much on an old beater 4X4). That amp is still holding up and still sounds good. Even my buddy who used to sell car stereos was semi-impressed. Lots of opinions and brand loyality with car audio.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Philphine||12-22-2000||05:37 PM||||like batboy, i used acouple of borderline amps in my truck, 6 channel jensens. it's actually the best system i've ever installed sound wise. if you have the amp already, work with it and maybe look for a better amp with similar dementions/features for the future if needed.<P>by the way, if someone has one of these (6 ch. 4x50+2x15) i'd be interested in buying it for my cheap thrill fiero.<p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 12-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||sombre||12-22-2000||11:06 PM||||  I had 2 amps built into an enclosure where the spare tire went up front.Not the flat cover like most people use.this was a round tray that had a plexi-glass cover over it.put it right in in place of the tire even used the factory hold down to hold it in place.I put the spare in the trunk with salvaged and modified mounts from annother car.<P>Wish I still had the box cuz it was tough to make and I need it again now for my '86.<P>  Don't worry about the amp.I have not tried sony for a while but they used to be real clean just not real powerful for the rated wattage.90% of amp failures have nothing to do with the amp itself.I've only ever had one go bad out of probably 30 or 40 that I've had over the years and all it was was a dirty relay...actually produced that same would not stay on prob. described above.I replaced the relay and the amp is still working today...Amp was made probably in 1990 or 91.<P>                                    greg||||reg||1
Z||000013||Seanh||12-23-2000||10:03 AM||||I have to agree, Sony Amps are not a good deal. We have had a little trouble with them here, but not enough for me to disreguard them. They put out very little power for the money. I highly reccomend Pioneer/Premier brand, very good quality and a fair amount of power. With Pioneer/Premier, you get what you pay for, no more, no less. For the money/wattage I would say AudioBahn. They are good quality, put out alot of power, and look really nice. I would also have to say mount them behind the seats or in a pod on top of the spare tire.||||reg||1
Z||000014||MachineHead||12-23-2000||08:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by william_thorniii:<BR><B>Machinehead,<P>Regarding your second proposal, of locating an amp on the center console.  Do you think it would be safe to locate an amplifier on top of the ECM?  I would worry about the EMF making the car go screwy.  <P>The only important thing about amplifier placement is to ensure that the area is well ventilated.  If you seal it up in an enclosure, make sure you use a powered exhaust fans (like in your computer) in the enclosure.<P>William T. Thorn, III</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The ECM is located in the back wall on a Fiero?  I thought it was in the dash somewhere. (sorry I don't own a Fiero, yet)  Actually, there wouldn't be enough interference to screw anything else as long as you have some sort of boundary between the two.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Monkeyman||12-23-2000||11:59 PM||||MachineHead--I don't know your experience with either cars or stereos, but listen to WTIII.  He knows both very well.  He's giving me some pointers on my stereo install.  (Sony CDX-M650 head unit, JBL 5 1/4" components, Kenwood amp, powered Bazooka tube)||||reg||1
Z||000016||MachineHead||12-24-2000||04:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Monkeyman:<BR><B>MachineHead--I don't know your experience with either cars or stereos, but listen to WTIII.  He knows both very well.  He's giving me some pointers on my stereo install.  (Sony CDX-M650 head unit, JBL 5 1/4" components, Kenwood amp, powered Bazooka tube)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I wasn't doubting him in any way.  I've just never had a problem, personally, with interference before, I never said it couldn't happen.  Car experience for me =bad.  Stereo Experience = rahter good. <BR>btw, good luck with your install. ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Alex2004||12-25-2000||02:10 PM||||My Dad just bought me a $220 Sony Xplod CD player for my Fiero, it's 50w X 4. Is it just the Sony AMP's that go to crap or do the CD players also go to crap after a year?  Let me know, thanks.<P>Alex...<p>[This message has been edited by Alex2004 (edited 12-25-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000018||Seanh||12-25-2000||03:01 PM||||Well, I dont know about Sony amps going bad after a year, I just dont think they provide good power for the money you pay. I think the headunits are okay. You probally wont have any trouble with it.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"It's always in the last place you look." Of course it is. Why would you keep looking after you've found it? Do people do this? Who and where are they?</I>||||reg||1
Z||000019||LarryB||12-25-2000||08:54 PM||||My personal experience with Sony equipment has been pretty negative, it just doesn't stand up, especially when compared with the other equipment I've owned and still own over the years.  Out of the 4 pieces of Sony equipment I'd bought in a 4-5 year period (TV, auto CD, home CD, 8mm camcorder) only the camcorder lasted more than a year past the warranty period.  The TV was especially galling because it was less than 2 years old (1 yr warranty) when the tuner module failed; it was cheaper to go out and buy a new TV than repair it.  I now refuse to buy any Sony products and actively encourage others to look elsewhere.  Just my $0.02.<P>I've been happy with Pioneer and have had especially good luck with Kenwood. IMO you wouldn't go wrong with either one. ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Seanh||12-26-2000||01:40 PM||||After hearing all the horror stories about Sony, I would tend to say stay away from them. I have heard not so good things about their products, but this is more then I thought. I was looking at the Audio Forum archieves about their ideas on Sony products. They mostly reccomend not useing Sony if you can help it. I too highly reccomend Pioneer/Premier and Kenwood is also great.||||reg||1
